00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.469 align:start position:0%
fighting tax evasion and fraud is high  on the EU finance ministers political  agenda today at their meeting in  Brussels they agreed to introduce a  binding antabuse clause in the parent  subsidiary directive this directive aims  at ensuring that profits made by  companies based in more than one member  state are not taxed twice but it has  been abused by certain corporate groups  to avoid paying taxes in any member  state this clause will prevent misuse of  the directive and ensure greater  consistency in its application to  different member states to help member  states clamp down on tax fraud and tax  evasion ministers also decided to extend  the scope of the automatic exchange of  information between tax authorities to  include interest dividends and account  balances the dual aim of this measure is  to prevent taxpayers from hiding capital  abroad or from hiding assets on which  tax is due whilst also improving the  efficiency of tax collection a third  important agreement was reached to  implement regulation defining the  contributions and be paid by banks to  the EU single resolution fund the fund  is to be used in cases of bank failure  and it's a key element of the single  resolution mechanism finally ministers  also discussed the Commission's 315  billion euro investment plan to  kick-start the European economy over the  next three years the plan will be  submitted to EU leaders for approval  when they meet next week in Brussels  director
 

